 Melno which permanently altered the geopolitical map of Central Europe by ceding key territories and diminishing the Order's power.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-10-03T07:03:56Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#37103111327", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Michael Küchmeister von Sternberg was the 28th Grand Master of the Teutonic Knights, serving from 1414 to 1422.", "newValue": "Michael Küchmeister von Sternberg (1370–1423) served as the 28th Grand Master of the Teutonic Knights from 1414 to 1422, inheriting an Order in profound crisis following the catastrophic defeat at the Battle of Grunwald in 1410 and the onerous terms of the First Peace of Thorn in 1411. His tenure was defined by the immense, and ultimately unsuccessful, challenge of rebuilding the Order's shattered treasury and military strength while navigating the complex, often hostile, diplomatic landscape of Central Europe, particularly with the ascendant Polish-Lithuanian Union.

Ascending to the Grand Mastership after the deposition of Heinrich von Plauen, Küchmeister inherited an institution burdened by crippling war indemnities to Poland-Lithuania, internal revolts among its subjects, and a severely tarnished international reputation. He pursued policies of aggressive financial consolidation, implementing new taxes, selling off valuable assets, and attempting to restore the Order's military capabilities through recruitment and fortification. Despite these efforts, his reign culminated in the brief but strategically significant Gollub War (1422) against Poland-Lithuania, which, despite some localized successes, ended with the signing of the Treaty of Melno, further eroding the Order's territorial integrity and political leverage.

